WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.600
Speaker 1: Hey, David, if Trump runs third
party, do you think he can win any states?

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.020
This is a really interesting question, because
I think the answer is maybe.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.020
Yes.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:16.990
I mean, listen, if you if you look at a variety
of different states and you poll Republicans

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.370
and you say how many of you would vote for
Trump if he ran?

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:25.550
Again, for the most part, Trump has more than
50 percent Republican support.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:28.010
I think in every state I may be wrong.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.750
There may be some state where Trump wouldn't
have 50 percent Republican support.

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:38.140
The math gets more complicated when you realize
that a Trump Republican nomination in twenty

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:47.589
four and a Trump third party run in twenty
twenty four would differently affect Republicans,

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:48.589
Independents and Democrats.

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:50.420
So let's take it one by one.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.730
If Trump is the Republican nominee, I think
that that will motivate more Democrats to

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.670
vote against him than if Trump runs as an
independent, because Democrats will see Trump

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.820
as an independent as less of a threat than
Trump as a Republican.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:15.640
OK, if Trump is the Republican nominee, I
think that independents will go more away

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.450
from Donald Trump, where if his Trump is,
whereas Trump if Trump is an independent or

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:25.960
third party candidate, it might actually attract
some independents who otherwise would have

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.400
said, no, no, no, this is not for me.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.390
I'm gonna vote for the Democrat and then for
Republicans.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.689
And this is where it gets really hard to predict.

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:37.549
If Trump is the Republican nominee in twenty
twenty four, I think he has almost all Republican

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:38.549
support.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:40.439
Every poll is indicative of that.

00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:45.700
But if Trump runs as an independent, about
60 percent of Republicans in a poll a couple

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:51.249
of months ago said they would follow Trump
to a third party or an independent status.

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:53.830
I don't know that I believe them.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.030
I genuinely am unsure that they would actually
follow through with that.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.450
So all of these are complicated scenarios.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:03.189
Now, could Trump as a third party candidate
win some states?

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:04.869
Yeah, I think it's possible.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:06.790
I think it's possible that he would.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:10.660
But the real question is, what does it do
in terms of getting to 270?

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:15.560
And to me, it seems abundantly clear that
the math of a Trump third party run points

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:21.040
one way and one way only, which is a massive
Democratic victory in particular.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:27.330
If because remember, scenario one is that
you've got a Democratic candidate which who

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:35.890
wins the normal blue states and then the Republican
nominee and third party, Trump split the red

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:36.890
states.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.670
OK, that's that's one scenario.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.860
But even without winning a single state, Donald
Trump as a third party candidate could absolutely

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.170
